<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; line-height: 1.6;">

    <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>
    
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Managing Stress in Changing Times</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Breathing to Relax</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze authentic non-fiction texts. Reading: Understand the relationship between main points and examples in various texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can compare examples from two wellness articles, evaluating how different stress-management techniques impact mental health (e.g., "Guided meditation may work better for beginners, while mindfulness is effective for daily stress relief."). Can understand the connections between the main points of an article on stress-relief methods and examples of their impact on mental health (e.g., "Deep breathing exercises calm the body and mind, reducing anxiety."). Can recognize examples that illustrate wellness practices in informational texts, using these to support personal wellness plans (e.g., "Listening to calming music can help me focus during study sessions.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>
    
    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; font-size: 20px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images on the board or projector showing various relaxation techniques (e.g., a person deep breathing, someone listening to music with headphones, a forest scene, someone meditating). Students are asked to identify the activities and share if they practice any of these to manage stress. The teacher encourages use of vocabulary like "breathing," "mindfulness," "relaxation," "music," and "meditation." (5 min)</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces a short paragraph (2-3 sentences) about the benefits of deep breathing. "Deep breathing is a simple yet effective technique for stress relief. Practicing deep breathing can calm your mind and body. You should try it." The teacher reads the paragraph aloud, modeling fluent reading and emphasizing the pronunciation of "soothing" (if used in the text) and the rhythm of "beneficial." The teacher points out the gerund "practicing" and the modal "should." (5 min)</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is deep breathing a technique for?" (Expected answer: "Stress relief.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'practicing' deep breathing help you do?" (Expected answer: "Calm your mind and body.")</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher confirms understanding of key terms like "calm," "mind," and "body."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Authentic non-fiction text: An online wellness article titled "Breathe Easy: Simple Techniques for Stress Relief in Changing Times" (approx. 250-300 words, B1.2 level). The article includes sections on deep breathing, guided meditation, and listening to soothing music, and explicitly uses gerunds, modals for advice, and present continuous for trends, along with target vocabulary like "mindfulness," "relaxation," "focus," "beneficial," "calming," "reduce," "improve," and "stress-free."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Analyze and interpret informational texts on health and wellness topics, identifying main ideas and supporting details to understand stress management techniques.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article individually for 2 minutes to get the general idea. Then, they answer a multiple-choice question: "What is the main purpose of this article? a) To promote a specific meditation app. b) To inform readers about various stress-relief techniques. c) To discuss the history of mindfulness. d) To describe different types of music." (3 min)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements related to the main points of the article. E.g., "Deep breathing exercises solely benefit the body, not the mind." (False). "Many people are practicing mindfulness to stay calm during stressful times." (True). "Listening to calming music can help improve your focus." (True). (5 min)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students match headings (e.g., "The Power of Deep Breathing," "Melodies for a Calm Mind") to paragraphs in the article. (3 min)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 11 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying main points and supporting examples in non-fiction texts. Grammar: Gerunds (e.g., "Practicing mindfulness"), Modals for advice (e.g., "You should try"), Present continuous for trends (e.g., "People are practicing"). Vocabulary: breathing, relaxation, mindfulness, meditation, stress, wellness, techniques, practices, mind, body, balance, relief, soothing, beneficial, calming, effective, focus, improve, reduce, manage.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Gap-fill with Focus Words:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences extracted from the article, containing gaps where key vocabulary or grammatical structures (gerunds, modals, present continuous) should be. Students complete the sentences using words from a provided box. E.g., "____ mindfulness has helped many people improve their focus." (Practicing). "You ____ try spending 10 minutes meditating." (should). "People ____ practicing various relaxation techniques." (are). This reinforces understanding of vocabulary and grammar in context. (5 min)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled – Main Idea & Example Identification:</b> In pairs, students re-read the article, highlighting or underlining the main point of each paragraph and one supporting example for it. For instance, for a paragraph about deep breathing, the main point might be "Deep breathing exercises calm the body and mind," and an example could be "reducing anxiety." The teacher guides them, saying, "Remember, the main point tells us *what* the paragraph is about, and the example shows us *how* or *why*." (6 min)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction & Peer Check:</b> Pairs compare their highlighted main points and examples. They discuss any discrepancies, politely offering suggestions for clarification using target language. E.g., "You could try looking at the first sentence again for the main idea of this section." "I think this example supports that point because it explains the benefit." The teacher circulates, facilitating discussion and noting pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "mindfulness" /ˈmaɪnd.fəl.nəs/ and "beneficial" /ˌben.ɪˈfɪʃ.əl/. (4 min)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Collaborative Text Analysis and Comparison.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4) to read two short excerpts (or distinct sections of a longer text) from different wellness articles (provided by the teacher) focusing on stress management. One excerpt could highlight "Guided Meditation" and the other "Mindful Movement" (e.g., gentle exercise like stretching or yoga). Each group's task is to compare the techniques by identifying the main points and examples for each, and then verbally evaluating how they might impact mental health.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Each group prepares a short (2-3 minute) oral report for the class, comparing the two techniques. They should use target vocabulary (e.g., "guided meditation," "mindful movement," "focus," "relaxation," "stress relief," "beneficial," "effective"), grammar (gerunds: "Practicing mindful movement," modals for advice: "You should try both," present continuous: "More people are discovering mindful movement"), and sociolinguistic functions (offering polite suggestions, sharing personal experiences). E.g., "Guided meditation may work better for beginners, while mindful movement is effective for daily stress relief." "In our experience, listening to calming music can help me focus during study sessions, but some people find participating in mindful exercise more beneficial."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The comparison directly relates to "Breathing to Relax" (as a component of meditation/mindful movement) and "Managing Stress in Changing Times" by evaluating different wellness strategies. Emphasis is placed on demonstrating understanding of the texts and their practical application.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 12 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on reading comprehension (identifying main points and examples), collaborative skills, and use of target language during the group comparison reports.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group orally presents their comparison of the two stress-management techniques, explaining the main points and supporting examples from each text, and offering their evaluation. The teacher listens for accurate summary of the texts.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies main points from both texts clearly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provides relevant examples to support main points.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., "mindfulness," "relaxation," "beneficial," "calming," "effective").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Applies target grammar (gerunds, modals for advice, present continuous for trends).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates intelligible pronunciation, including /uː/ and full/reduced vowels.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates effectively and respectfully within the group.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Presents a coherent comparison of the techniques.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short reflection:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new stress-management technique you learned about today. How does it work?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you in identifying the main idea and supporting examples in a text? (1-5 scale)"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 7 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading and analyzing the wellness articles was most challenging for you today, and why?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategy did you use today to help you understand the relationship between main points and examples in the texts?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students analyze the texts and identify main points/examples? (e.g., the guided highlighting, peer discussion).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with text analysis or integrating the target grammar/vocabulary? (e.g., providing sentence frames for comparisons, more explicit modeling of connecting examples to main ideas).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the pronunciation practice (/uː/ and full/reduced vowels) effectively integrated?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on reading and understanding different stress-management techniques. In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will actively discuss these techniques and share personal experiences and advice with classmates, using the main points and examples they analyzed today as a basis for their conversations. They will practice giving advice on stress management and sharing relaxation techniques.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>